# local-weather-awareness: environment for make_weather_page.py (every WEATHER_* variable
# read by weather/config.py, with its default). Format is systemd EnvironmentFile: KEY=value
# lines, no `export`, no surrounding quotes, `#` comments (the cron wrapper also tolerates
# blanks around the `=`). Command-line flags (--out, --cache, --sites, --verbose) override
# these.
#
# Two files are read, in this order (later wins): /etc/local-weather-awareness.env, then
# ~<run user>/local-weather-awareness.env. The cron wrapper
# (deploy/local-weather-awareness-cron.sh, used on tau.kirx.net: Gentoo/OpenRC, no systemd)
# parses them literally before every run, never sources them; the systemd unit (other
# hosts) reads them as EnvironmentFile=. Install with (as root)
#   cp weather.env.example /etc/local-weather-awareness.env
#   chmod 644 /etc/local-weather-awareness.env
# and rerun the installer (deploy/install-cron.sh, or deploy/install.sh under systemd) after
# changing WEATHER_OUT_DIR or WEATHER_CACHE_DIR (it creates the directories). Set those two
# in /etc/local-weather-awareness.env only: the installer runs as root and refuses paths
# from a file the run user can write (~<run user>/local-weather-awareness.env).
# Everything is optional and every line here is commented out, so the defaults apply: the
# installers then write the output directory they chose into
# /etc/local-weather-awareness.env (/var/www/localhost/htdocs/myweather on Gentoo,
# /var/www/html/myweather elsewhere).
#
# ABSOLUTELY CRUCIAL: set WEATHER_USER_AGENT with YOUR REAL E-MAIL ADDRESS (see below).
# With a placeholder such as you@example.org left in, OpenStreetMap blocks every map tile
# request (the maps show an "Access blocked" tile) and the generator refuses to start.
# (A '%' is literal in an EnvironmentFile; only in a unit's Environment= line it must be '%%'.)

# ---- sites -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# With neither variable set, the built-in list is used: the example deployment's sites,
# the same list as sites.example. Choose your own in one of two ways (the first that is set
# wins; --sites on the command line beats both):
# 1. WEATHER_SITES: slug|Display name|lat|lon[|tz] entries separated by ';', e.g.
#    denver|Denver, CO|39.7392|-104.9903;flagstaff|Flagstaff, AZ|35.1983|-111.6513
# 2. WEATHER_SITES_FILE: a file with the same entries, one per line, '#' comments allowed;
#    see sites.example (an absolute path, e.g. /etc/local-weather-awareness/sites). An
#    unreadable or malformed file is a startup error (exit status 2), never skipped.
# slug = [A-Za-z0-9_-], unique without regard to case (used in file names and #site-<slug>
# anchors). Lat/lon in decimal degrees, US longitudes negative: a site whose map reaches no
# US state or territory, or whose map would cross the 180th meridian, is a startup error.
# tz (optional) = IANA time zone, used for local times whenever NWS /points metadata (the
# usual source of the zone) is unavailable; without it such times fall back to UTC. An
# unknown zone name is a startup error. The NWS grid, zones, time zone, alert areas, maps
# and page title follow the sites; road closures exist only for maps in New Mexico, and
# radar only in the contiguous US.
# WEATHER_SITES=
# WEATHER_SITES_FILE=

# ---- paths -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where index.html, status.json and radar_<slug>_<theme>.png are written (Apache serves it).
# Without it the generator writes ./out; the installers fill it in: Gentoo's DocumentRoot
# gives /var/www/localhost/htdocs/myweather (tau.kirx.net), Debian/RHEL /var/www/html/myweather.
# WEATHER_OUT_DIR=/var/www/localhost/htdocs/myweather
# Disk cache: NWS JSON, zone shapes, basemap composites, last radar frame, the run lock.
# WEATHER_CACHE_DIR=~/.cache/local-weather-awareness
# Single-instance lock file (default: <WEATHER_CACHE_DIR>/run.lock).
# WEATHER_LOCK_FILE=

# ---- page ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Browser tab title and masthead. Empty = derived from the site names: "Local weather: "
# and each name up to its first comma, joined with " · " ("+N more" past 80 characters).
# WEATHER_TITLE=
# The page's public address, shown in the footer, e.g. https://<your host>/myweather/
# (the example deployment has https://tau.kirx.net/myweather/). Empty: no address shown.
# WEATHER_PAGE_URL=
# Page auto-reload interval in seconds (minimum 30; keep it >= the timer period). The page
# reloads itself with JavaScript, keeping open sections and the scroll position; a
# <noscript> meta refresh does the same for browsers without JavaScript.
# WEATHER_REFRESH_SECONDS=90
# "us" = °F/mph primary with metric in small print; "metric" = the reverse.
# WEATHER_UNITS=us
# Rows in the hourly table.
# WEATHER_HOURLY_HOURS=24
# Day/night periods in the 7-day strip (NWS returns 14).
# WEATHER_FORECAST_PERIODS=14
# Precipitation probability (percent) at/above which a table cell is highlighted.
# WEATHER_POP_HIGHLIGHT_PCT=30
# Show the sunrise/sunset/twilight row per site (1/0, true/false, yes/no, on/off).
# WEATHER_SHOW_SUN=1

# ---- HTTP ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# api.weather.gov REQUIRES a User-Agent, and NWS asks for a contact in it. The default
# names only the project, so a deployment should add its operator's e-mail address after
# the project URL, separated by "; ", e.g.
#   local-weather-awareness (+https://github.com/kirxkirx/local-weather-awareness; you@example.org)
# with you@example.org REPLACED BY YOUR REAL E-MAIL ADDRESS. This is crucial: without a real
# address the OpenStreetMap tiles cannot be fetched (OSM blocks User-Agents with placeholder
# contacts such as you@example.org or CONTACT_EMAIL, and the generator refuses to start).
# WEATHER_USER_AGENT=local-weather-awareness (+https://github.com/kirxkirx/local-weather-awareness)
# Per-request timeout in seconds.
# WEATHER_HTTP_TIMEOUT=25
# Retries after a network error or a 5xx/429 response.
# WEATHER_HTTP_RETRIES=2
# Network time budget of one run, in seconds (minimum 10). Once it is spent, every further
# fetch is skipped and the page is built from last-good copies (labelled stale); a host that
# times out or refuses connections after its retries is skipped for the rest of that run.
# Keep it below the 2-minute run interval (cron / timer) and the unit's TimeoutStartSec=10min.
# WEATHER_RUN_BUDGET=90

# ---- NWS caching (seconds): _TTL = re-fetch after; _MAX_STALE = keep showing last-good until
# /points metadata (grid, zones, time zone, station list) almost never changes.
# WEATHER_POINTS_TTL=604800
# 7-day forecast.
# WEATHER_FORECAST_TTL=1800
# WEATHER_FORECAST_MAX_STALE=7200
# Hourly forecast.
# WEATHER_HOURLY_TTL=1800
# WEATHER_HOURLY_MAX_STALE=7200
# Station observations.
# WEATHER_OBS_TTL=600
# WEATHER_OBS_MAX_STALE=3600

# ---- alerts ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# States, territories and marine areas whose active alerts are fetched (api.weather.gov
# ?area= list). auto = every state, territory and NWS marine area (LM = Lake Michigan,
# PZ = Pacific off the West Coast, ...) whose bounding box touches a site's map (the run
# log names them). Codes listed with auto are added (auto,PK); without auto only the listed
# codes are queried, e.g. NM,TX, and a site in a state the list leaves out is reported in
# the log and in status.json (its alerts are not fetched).
# WEATHER_ALERT_AREAS=auto
# The alert feed is fetched every run; on failure the last good copy is shown this long.
# WEATHER_ALERTS_MAX_STALE=1200
# Opacity (0..255) of the alert-area fill on the maps (outline is always solid).
# WEATHER_ALERT_FILL_ALPHA=55
# Per-event colour overrides for the maps and the page: 'Event Name=#rrggbb' entries
# separated by ';'. Event names as NWS sends them, matched case-insensitively. They replace
# the built-in palette (the NWS chart, except inland flood products, which are drawn in reds
# instead of the chart's greens). A malformed entry is a startup error. Example:
# WEATHER_ALERT_COLORS=Flood Watch=#E53935;Red Flag Warning=#FF1493

# ---- radar map -------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Map width AND height, statute miles, centred on each site (10..1000).
# WEATHER_MAP_MILES=100
# Output width in pixels (200..2000); height follows the Mercator aspect (about square).
# WEATHER_MAP_PX=600
# Basemap tile zoom (5..12): 9 = about 1.5 km/px before downsampling; 10 doubles the tiles.
# WEATHER_TILE_ZOOM=9
# Tile URL templates ({z}/{x}/{y}); default OpenStreetMap standard tiles for both themes
# (CARTO's free rasters now return an "API KEY REQUIRED" tile). Any {z}/{x}/{y} raster works.
# WEATHER_TILE_URL_DARK=https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png
# WEATHER_TILE_URL_LIGHT=https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png
# Dark theme: invert the lightness (hue kept) of each tile that comes out light; 0 = use
# the tiles as served (for a genuinely dark tile set).
# WEATHER_TILE_DARK_INVERT=1
# Set to 0 to skip the MRMS radar layer entirely (maps still show basemap + alerts).
# WEATHER_RADAR=1
# Echoes below this reflectivity (dBZ) are not drawn.
# WEATHER_RADAR_DBZ_MIN=5
# Opacity (0..255) of the radar layer.
# WEATHER_RADAR_ALPHA=170
# How many 2-minute frames to look back for the newest available MRMS image.
# WEATHER_RADAR_MAX_BACK=10
# Reuse the last good frame (labelled STALE) this long if the archive is unreachable.
# WEATHER_RADAR_MAX_STALE=1800
# strftime pattern of the MRMS composite-reflectivity PNG archive (Iowa Environmental Mesonet).
# WEATHER_MRMS_ARCHIVE=https://mesonet.agron.iastate.edu/archive/data/%Y/%m/%d/GIS/mrms/lcref_%Y%m%d%H%M.png

# ---- road closures (New Mexico only, emergencies only) ---------------------------------
# NMDOT road closures caused by an emergency (flooding, washouts, rock/mud slides, fire,
# crashes, hazmat, snow/ice, wind/dust, storm damage, law enforcement) are listed per site
# and drawn on the maps. Roadwork, construction and lane closures, scheduled/nightly and
# seasonal closures, special events, convoys, White Sands missile-range alerts and rest-area
# closures are left out. Other states' roads are not covered: a map outside New Mexico says
# so, and when no site's map reaches New Mexico nothing is fetched and no road block is
# shown. 1/0.
# WEATHER_ROADS=1
# Also list and draw "water on road" (NMDOT driving conditions reporting flooding, standing
# water, water over the road or an impassable road).
# WEATHER_ROADS_WATER=1
# Also list and draw NWS Local Storm Reports from New Mexico that name a closed, flooded or
# impassable road, crossing or arroyo (via the Iowa Environmental Mesonet), shown as dated
# reports that may have reopened.
# WEATHER_LSR=1
# Storm-report window in hours (1..168).
# WEATHER_LSR_HOURS=24
# When a road source is down, keep showing its last good copy this long (seconds, 0..604800).
# WEATHER_ROADS_MAX_STALE=3600
# Flag an NMDOT item "may have reopened" when it has not been updated for this many days.
# WEATHER_ROADS_OLD_DAYS=3
# Sources (http:// or https://). {hours} in the storm-report URL is replaced by
# WEATHER_LSR_HOURS; IEM filters by state with states=NM (a wfos= list is ignored by IEM).
# The cron wrapper takes values literally, so the '&' needs no quoting there; if this file is
# ever sourced by a shell instead, quote the storm-report URL: an unquoted '&' ends the command.
# WEATHER_NMROADS_JSON_URL=https://nmroads.com/nmroads.json
# WEATHER_NMROADS_RSS_URL=https://nmroads.com/rss.xml
# WEATHER_LSR_URL=https://mesonet.agron.iastate.edu/geojson/lsr.geojson?hours={hours}&states=NM

# ---- misc ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DEBUG logging to stderr/journal (same as --verbose).
# WEATHER_VERBOSE=0
